      "text": "The given information tells us that we have a sample of oxygen that is initially at a volume of \\(V_1 = 2.28\\) liters and is subjected to a pressure of \\(P_1 = 5\\) kPa. We want to determine the new pressure \\(P_2\\) when the oxygen is transferred to a new volume of \\(V_2 = 5.7\\) liters at the same temperature. Due to the relationship between volume, pressure, and temperature, which we can express as \\(PV = k\\), where \\(k\\) is a constant, we can use this relationship to solve the problem.\n\nFirst, let's find the value of \\(k\\).\n\nGiven \\(P_1 \\cdot V_1 = k\\), we can solve for \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\nk = P_1 \\cdot V_1 = 5 \\, \\text{kPa} \\cdot 2.28 \\, \\text{L}\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find the new pressure \\(P_2\\) when the volume is changed to \\(V_2\\):\n\n\\[\nP_2 \\cdot V_2 = k\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(k\\) and \\(V_2\\) into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\nP_2 \\cdot 5.7 \\, \\text{L} = 5 \\, \\text{kPa} \\cdot 2.28 \\, \\text{L}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(P_2\\):\n\n\\[\nP_2 = \\frac{5 \\, \\text{kPa} \\cdot 2.28 \\, \\text{L}}{5.7 \\, \\text{L}}\n\\]\n\nNow we can do the calculation using Python to ensure the accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\nP1 = 5  # initial pressure in kPa\nV1 = 2.28  # initial volume in liters\nV2 = 5.7  # new volume in liters\n\n# Calculate k\nk = P1 * V1\n\n# Calculate new pressure P2\nP2 = k / V2\nP2_rounded = round(P2, 1)  # rounding to 1 decimal place for better readability\nprint(P2_rounded)\n```\n```output\n2.0\n```\nThe new pressure \\(P_2\\) when the oxygen is transferred to a volume of 5.7 liters at the same temperature is \\(\\boxed{2.0}\\) kPa.",
